<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Method correctness: fairness diagnostics</h3>

<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Acceptance vs. length and LRE.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further examine acceptance <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(L)</annotation></semantics></math> by length bins and report the Length Reweighting Error (LRE) for each method (Figure <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Acceptance vs. length and LRE. ‣ 5.2 Method correctness: fairness diagnostics ‣ 5 Results ‣ Clip Your Sequences Fairly: Enforcing Length Fairness for Sequence-Level RL" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). FSPO equalizes <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(L)</annotation></semantics></math> across lengths, yielding a markedly smaller LRE consistent with the directional guarantee (Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 (Directional guarantee under length fairness). ‣ Setup. ‣ 2 Length Fairness and Length Reweighting Error (LRE) ‣ Clip Your Sequences Fairly: Enforcing Length Fairness for Sequence-Level RL" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). Slightly higher values in the shortest-length bins are expected due to small sample sizes and occasional outliers of abnormally short sequences. The computed LRE is <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.162" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.162</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.162</annotation></semantics></math> for RLOO, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.264" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.264</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.264</annotation></semantics></math> for GSPO, and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.037" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.037</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.037</annotation></semantics></math> for FSPO, where FSPO achieves significant better (smaller) LRE among all.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09177v1/clipratio_length.svg" id="S5.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/230;" width="405" height="230"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Acceptance probability vs. length; FSPO achieves low LRE and flattens acceptance across bins.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Effectiveness: learning dynamics and downstream</h3>

<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mean Response length.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Mean Response length. ‣ 5.3 Effectiveness: learning dynamics and downstream ‣ 5 Results ‣ Clip Your Sequences Fairly: Enforcing Length Fairness for Sequence-Level RL" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, both RLOO and FSPO learn quickly and increase response length in the early stage. However, RLOO’s response length later explodes to very large values, with much of the extra content being filler. A plausible explanation is that longer sequences are more likely to be clipped in RLOO; consequently, the negative signal from long and incorrect answers is suppressed, so the model fails to learn to regulate length. By contrast, GSPO learns more slowly at the beginning and struggles to increase length, especially for the weaker 1.7B model. On the 8B model, FSPO achieves the best performance with a shorter average length, suggesting more balanced learning across the length distribution and more effective control and use of length.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.09177v1/figs/length1.7b.png" id="S5.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">1.7B</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.09177v1/figs/length8b.png" id="S5.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">8B</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mean Response Length during Training</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We revisited sequence-level clipping and identified a length-reweighting pathology: fixed clip ranges at the sequence level systematically bias acceptance across response lengths and distort the effective objective. We formalized <em id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">length fairness</em> via a Length Reweighting Error (LRE) and proved that small LRE preserves the update direction through a cosine guarantee. Motivated by an asymptotic Gaussian law for the sequence log-IS ratio, we proposed FSPO, which enforces fairness by clipping in log-IS space with a KL-corrected drift and a <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>L</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{L}</annotation></semantics></math>-scaled band, thereby keeping IS semantics intact while equalizing acceptance across lengths. Empirically, on math-reasoning benchmarks and two model scales, FSPO flattens acceptance-by-length, stabilizes training dynamics, and delivers consistent gains over all baselines. In future work, we plan extend the training and evaluation of FSPO beyond math to broader RLVR suites.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
